# EXISTING FIGMA DESIGN — EXTENSION MODE

You are working on an existing, completed Figma design.
Figma design link-https://www.figma.com/design/CebX98K0BKepBsWee0uE17/Untitled?node-id=0-1&m=dev&t=YayqdxWAr6MjmB69-1

Your job is NOT to recreate, redesign, replace or reinterpret the existing design.

Your job is to EXTEND the existing design by adding new features, sections, components and pages.

Strcitly Maintain:-
1)Implementation_log.md
2)Project_status.md 
3)Tests_performed.md(Stores all tests performed and passed)
Make changes and maintain these files after every Work(prompt) is completed and also commitfile regularly


## 1. Preserve the existing design language

Before making changes, inspect the relevant existing Figma frames.

Understand and reuse:
- Existing colour palette.
- Typography and font hierarchy.
- Spacing system.
- Border radii.
- Shadows.
- Button styles.
- Card styles.
- Navigation patterns.
- Iconography.
- Component conventions.
- Responsive behaviour.

## 2. New additions

Every new element must look like it belongs to the existing design.

Do not introduce an unrelated visual style.

Do not arbitrarily change existing components to accommodate new additions.

## 3. Existing functionality

Preserve all existing functionality and layouts.

Do not delete, replace or restructure existing components without explicit permission.

## 4. Before implementation

First inspect the existing project and Figma design.

Then:
1. Identify reusable components.
2. Identify existing design tokens.
3. Determine where the requested addition belongs.
4. Explain your proposed implementation.
5. Wait for approval before making substantial changes.

## 5. Quality requirements

- Maintain visual consistency.
- Use existing assets.
- Match existing responsive behaviour.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
- Keep components reusable.
- Test the new additions.
- Check that existing pages still work.

## MOST IMPORTANT RULE

Treat the existing Figma design as the visual authority.

You are extending an existing product, not designing a different product.